# ============================================================================
# Allinone Backend Configuration
# ============================================================================
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your actual values
# NEVER commit .env to version control

# ============================================================================
# Application
# ============================================================================
# Omitting APP_ENV is not development: with no value named, `APP_ENV` resolves to
# absent, so `ConfigurationService.isLocalDevelopment` is false and the process
# boots production-strict — `validateProductionSecrets()` refuses the template
# secrets below, `REDIS_URL` becomes required, and the OTP-to-stdout logging stays
# off. Both boot schemas used to carry `.default("development")` here, which made
# an unnamed Railway service indistinguishable from a laptop; development is now
# something you opt into by writing the word.
# So: local dev must set APP_ENV=development explicitly. Note that dotenv is
# last-wins — the *later* assignment in the loaded file is the one that counts, and
# the `.env` in this working copy declares `APP_ENV=production`. Copying this file
# over an existing `.env`, or appending to one, can therefore leave the value you
# did not mean; check the whole file for a second `APP_ENV=` line before chasing a
# gate that "should" be off.
APP_ENV=development
APP_PORT=3000
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# ============================================================================
# Database (MongoDB)
# ============================================================================
# Format: mongodb://host:port/database
# The server must run as replica set member (mongod --replSet rs0); transactions
# used by sync/vault services fail against a standalone instance.
DATABASE_URL="mongodb://localhost:27017/allinone_dev"

# ============================================================================
# Redis
# ============================================================================
# Also where the rate-limit counters live: `ThrottlerModule` uses
# `RedisThrottlerStorage` unless the process says it is a test run or a
# development one with no Redis named here
# (`ConfigurationService.usesRedisThrottlerStorage`). Outside those two the
# variable is required rather than defaulted — `localhost:6379` is a wrong guess
# inside a container, and the storage permits a request when its command fails,
# so a deployment that forgot this value would be running with no throttling at
# all while reporting itself healthy.
# Neither boot schema declares a default for it either, because a Joi default is
# written back into `process.env` and the "was `REDIS_URL` actually declared?"
# test behind that requirement would then only ever see a value. The localhost
# fallback lives in the `redisUrl` getter, which consumers read and the gate does
# not.
# In `docker-compose.prod.yml` the api and worker services get
# `redis://:<REDIS_PASSWORD>@redis:6379`: the `redis` service runs with
# `requirepass`, and it is reachable only on that file's `allinone-prod` network,
# which is the same reason none of the data services publish a port.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ============================================================================
# Cross-origin policy (HTTP API and the /sync WebSocket)
# ============================================================================
# Two separate lists, because they answer two separate handshakes.
#
# CORS_ORIGIN is the HTTP API's, read by app.enableCors() in src/main.ts. It
# defaults to http://localhost:3000 when unset. Comma-separated for several
# origins.
# CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
#
# WS_CORS_ORIGINS is the /sync namespace's, built at boot by SyncIoAdapter
# (src/sync/adapters/sync-io.adapter.ts). Unset, it falls through to
# CORS_ORIGINS, then CORS_ORIGIN, then the same http://localhost:3000 — so a
# deployment that configured the HTTP app is not left with an open socket. Set
# it when the web client is served from a different origin than the API.
#
# The list is never quietly widened: the only way a socket accepts any origin is
# to write * into it yourself, and * is then answered with credentials disabled
# (a browser rejects the two together).
# WS_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
#
# A request with no Origin header, or a literal `Origin: null`, is what a
# WebView-hosted or non-browser client sends. Off by default; the answer to "my
# web build cannot connect" is to name its origin above, not to switch this on.
# WS_CORS_ALLOW_NULL_ORIGIN=false
# Accept dynamically assigned localhost ports only during local development.
# Keep this false on every deployed service.
ALLOW_LOCALHOST_CORS=false
# Publish Swagger only from a trusted development environment.
SWAGGER_ENABLED=false
#
# Socket.IO rooms live in one process unless a pub/sub backplane joins them, so
# with more than one API replica a device on replica B misses what replica A
# emitted. Clustering is attempted when REDIS_URL is set and switched off by
# WS_REDIS_ADAPTER=false. A Redis that is configured but unreachable costs one
# connect timeout at boot and falls back to in-memory, like the throttler does.
# WS_REDIS_ADAPTER=true

# ============================================================================
# Authentication & JWT
# ============================================================================
# The REPLACE_WITH__…__DO_NOT_SHIP spellings below are refused at boot outside
# development, so copying this file is not a working key: generate one per value
# with `openssl rand -base64 48`.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH__openssl_rand_-base64_48__DO_NOT_SHIP
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRATION=15m
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH__a_different_random_value__DO_NOT_SHIP
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRATION=7d

# ============================================================================
# Admin access (/admin/*) — fails closed, opt in explicitly
# ============================================================================
# AdminGuard admits a caller only through the three values below, and an unset
# or empty value means NO admin access via that mechanism. There is
# deliberately no working example address here. Mailbox verification is
# required by default, but ADMIN_USER_IDS is still stronger because it does not
# depend on an address remaining assigned to the same person.
# Comma-separated verified emails, matched against the authenticated user.
# ADMIN_EMAILS=
# Comma-separated user ids (User.id), matched exactly.
# ADMIN_USER_IDS=
# Break-glass for operator tooling, sent as `x-admin-secret: <value>`. It
# escalates an already-authenticated request only — it is not an identity by
# itself — and the header path stays disabled unless the value is at least 32
# characters. Leave it commented to have no header path at all; a value you did
# write is held to the same length-and-entropy rule as the JWT pair in *every*
# environment, development included, because `GET /metrics` is answered on this
# secret alone (`src/common/metrics/metrics-scrape.guard.ts`).
# ADMIN_SECRET=

# ============================================================================
# Object Storage (S3-compatible)
# ============================================================================
OBJECT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
OBJECT_STORAGE_REGION=us-east-1
OBJECT_STORAGE_BUCKET=allinone-dev
OBJECT_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
OBJECT_STORAGE_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin
OBJECT_STORAGE_USE_SSL=false

# ============================================================================
# Email (SMTP / Gmail)
# ============================================================================
# For Gmail, turn on 2-Step Verification and create a Google App Password.
# Never use your normal Google account password here.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=yourname@gmail.com
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_FROM=yourname@gmail.com
SMTP_TLS=true
# Keep this enabled in every deployed environment. It may only be disabled for
# isolated local development where the application cannot send mail.
EMAIL_VERIFY_ENABLED=true
EMAIL_VERIFY_TOKEN_EXPIRY=24h

# ============================================================================
# Encryption (Application-level — generate a value; nothing here boots a deployment)
# ============================================================================
ENCRYPTION_KEY=REPLACE_WITH__openssl_rand_-base64_48__DO_NOT_SHIP

# The vault verifier pepper ring. ENCRYPTION_KEY has exactly two jobs: it derives
# the AES key that seals each user's TOTP secret
# (`src/auth/auth.service.ts:1376`), and it HMACs every stored `masterKeyHash`
# (the bare verifier is never kept). Changing it on its own therefore makes every
# existing vault reject its correct master password (and each attempt spends the
# unlock cooldown), and every TOTP secret sealed under the old value fails to
# decrypt. Rotation is two variables, in this order:
#   1. ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=<the value being retired>, before changing
#      ENCRYPTION_KEY. Rows sealed under the old value still unlock and are
#      re-sealed under the new one as they open.
#   2. Clear ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS once every vault has unlocked at least
#      once — otherwise the retired key stays live in the environment.
# The ring covers vaults only. `decryptTotpSecret` tries the current key and
# nothing else, so MFA users are NOT carried across a rotation: they must sign in
# with a recovery code or re-enrol, and a failed decrypt returns the ciphertext
# rather than erroring, which reads as "invalid code" forever. Read the rotation
# section of docs/DEPLOYMENT.md before doing this in production.
# Both _ID variables are optional labels stamped on rows; leaving them blank
# uses a fingerprint of the key, which is what lets a second rotation be
# distinguished from the first. Blanks below are the steady state, not a
# misconfiguration.
ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID=
ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=
ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS_ID=

# ============================================================================
# Rate Limiting
# ============================================================================
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=100
# Throttling is on by default, including during development. Set this false
# only for a short, local diagnostic session; never deploy it that way.
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
# Lets a request skip throttling with `x-skip-throttle: true`. Honoured outside
# production only, and off by default: a client must not choose its own limit.
RATE_LIMIT_HEADER_BYPASS=false

# ============================================================================
# Logging (Structured JSON to stdout, collected by Docker/journald/Loki)
# ============================================================================
LOG_FORMAT=json
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ============================================================================
# Monitoring (Prometheus - open-source, self-hosted)
# ============================================================================
# Prometheus scrapes the API's own `GET /metrics`, served on `APP_PORT`
# (`src/common/metrics/metrics.controller.ts`). The application opens no separate
# metrics listener, so there is no port to declare here and nothing reads one —
# a number written here would only invite a scrape config pointed at a process
# that is not listening.
METRICS_ENABLED=true

# ============================================================================
# OpenTelemetry (Optional - Jaeger, Loki integration)
# ============================================================================
OTEL_ENABLED=false
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318

# ============================================================================
# Backup Configuration
# ============================================================================
BACKUP_ENABLED=true
BACKUP_SCHEDULE=0 2 * * * # 2 AM daily
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# Read by scripts/backup-database.sh: when set, the dump is encrypted to
# *.archive.gz.enc and the plaintext archive is deleted - including when
# encryption itself fails. Restore needs the same value. Unset, the dump stays
# plaintext, which includes the VaultSetting collection.
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=
# Both scripts source this file first if it exists, and refuse it unless its
# mode has no group/other bits (chmod 600), so the passphrase never appears in
# a crontab, a shell history or `ps`.
BACKUP_ENV_FILE=/etc/allinone/backup.env
# Optional second location for the finished archive, verified with cmp and
# pruned on the same retention clock. Same-disk is not disaster recovery -
# point it at another volume or a mounted share.
# BACKUP_COPY_DIR=
# Nothing consumes BACKUP_ENABLED or BACKUP_SCHEDULE yet - see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md
# Step 8 for the cron line that runs the script on a schedule.


